Three of those cross\-checks compared the statistic to R\*(Aqs printed value relatively, and on the tables in question R\*(Aqs value is not a statistic. Where a 2×2 has all four |O−E| equal, Yates\*(Aq min(0.5, |O−E|) cancels every corrected residual, so the exact statistic is 0 and the exact p is 1; what R prints there — 1.4515367733818938e\-24 for [[1573,3],[4,0]], 2.9347503914472165e\-32 for [[1,2],[3,4]], 7.1842689582627857e\-32 for [[1.5,2.5],[3.5,4.5]] — is the leftover of forming E in floating point, the four |O−E| differing in their last bits so that the minimum comes out a hair below the rest. Its size is a property of the NV rather than of the test: a double build reproduces R\*(Aqs digits, and a _\|_float128 build cancels the whole way to 0. Comparing that relatively can only pass on the width R happened to use, and it failed with rel diff = 1 on the quadmath perl and on 5.12.5. Those three cases in t/chisq_test.R.scipy.t now check the statistic against 0 and the p\-value against 1 with absolute tolerances of 1e\-20 and 1e\-11, R\*(Aqs numbers staying in the file as provenance. LikeR.xs is unchanged — the wide\-NV answer was the more accurate one. The suite passes on perl 5.10.1, 5.12.5, 5.42.3\-thr, 5.44.0 and 5.44.0\-quadmath.
