\&\f(CW\*(C`fisher_test\*(C'\fR was cross\-validated against R 4.6.1 \f(CW\*(C`stats::fisher.test\*(C'\fR and SciPy
1.17.1 \f(CW\*(C`scipy.stats.fisher_exact\*(C'\fR using their own test suites rather than cases
invented here: SciPy\*(Aqs 84\-case R\-generated corpus
(\f(CW\*(C`scipy/stats/tests/data/fisher_exact_results_from_r.py\*(C'\fR, four numbers per case
over two confidence levels and all three alternatives), its
\&\f(CW\*(C`TestFisherExact\*(C'\fR, R\*(Aqs regression suite (\f(CW\*(C`tests/reg\-tests\-1{a,b,d,e}.R\*(C'\fR:
PR#644, PR#1662, PR#4688, PR#10558, PR#18336, PR#17671 and the "exact
fisher.test" entry) and the \f(CW\*(C`?fisher.test\*(C'\fR examples. They are in
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/fisher_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR. Three fixes came out of it:
.IP \(bu 4
The 2x2 hypergeometric density was built by differencing \f(CW\*(C`lgamma\*(C'\fR, which
costs the back half of a large table\*(Aqs p\-value: at a margin of 8.4e7,
\&\f(CW\*(C`lgamma\*(C'\fR is about 1.4e9, where a double\*(Aqs spacing is 2.4e\-7, and exponentiating
that turns into a relative error of the same size. SciPy\*(Aqs gh\-3014 case came
out right to only seven digits. The density is now assembled from Loader\*(Aqs
saddle\-point binomial, which is how R\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`dhyper\*(C'\fR avoids this and which
\&\f(CW\*(C`binom_test\*(C'\fR already had in the file; its three terms stay O(1) whatever the
margins are. Worst\-case agreement with R over the 84\-case corpus went from
2.1e\-12 to 5.2e\-14 relative, and gh\-3014 from 2.2e\-07 to 1.5e\-16.
.IP \(bu 4
The R x C enumeration charged only its leaves against its safety cap, so a
table wide enough to spend the time in the interior of the tree neither
finished nor stopped: R\*(Aqs PR#4688 table (4x3, N = 16442), whose whole point
upstream is that \f(CW\*(C`fisher.test\*(C'\fR must fail rather than return \f(CW\*(C`p = Inf\*(C'\fR, ran for
over five minutes here without doing either. Every node is now counted, and
that table is declined in about a second.
.IP \(bu 4
The R x C enumeration now bounds each subtree before walking it. \f(CWlgamma(x+1)\fR
is convex and \f(CW\*(C`a!b! <= (a+b)!\*(C'\fR, which together bracket the probability of every
completion of a partial table; when the whole subtree falls inside the tail its
mass is added in closed form (\f(CW\*(C`N\*(Aq! / (prod R_i! prod C_j!)\*(C'\fR, from counting the
remaining observations into rows two ways), and when it falls outside the
subtree is dropped. The margins are also transposed and sorted first, so the
fattest row and column are the ones the enumeration gets for free. R\*(Aqs Job
Satisfaction 4x4 example went from 7.5s to 0.3s and PR#644\*(Aqs 19x2 from 1.0s to
under 0.05s, and the 6x6 table of PR#18336 \-\- which segfaulted R before 4.2.0
and which R 4.6.1 still declines with \f(CW\*(C`hash key 5e+09 > INT_MAX\*(C'\fR \-\- is now
computable at 0.6322160531, agreeing with R\*(Aqs own 2e6\-replicate
\&\f(CW\*(C`simulate.p.value\*(C'\fR fallback to within its sampling error.
.PP
Two behaviours that the two references disagree about are now pinned by tests
rather than left to chance: a table with an empty row or column returns R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`p = 1\*(C'\fR with an odds ratio of 0 and a CI of (0, Inf), not SciPy\*(Aqs NaN odds ratio;
and a table with a single row or column is rejected as R rejects it, rather than
returning SciPy\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`p = 1\*(C'\fR.
