\&\f(CW\*(C`power_t_test\*(C'\fR was cross\-validated against R 4.6.1 \f(CW\*(C`power.t.test\*(C'\fR and against
\&\f(CW\*(C`scipy.stats.nct\*(C'\fR driven by \f(CW\*(C`scipy.optimize.brentq\*(C'\fR, over a grid of 288 cases
covering all five solved\-for parameters, all three types, both alternatives and
\&\f(CW\*(C`strict\*(C'\fR. Three fixes came out of it:
.IP \(bu 4
The Simpson sum behind the noncentral \fIt\fR CDF put a fixed 30000\-step grid on
\&\f(CW\*(C`u = w/(1+w)\*(C'\fR, and the chi density it integrates defeats that at both ends.
The density carries \f(CW\*(C`w**(df\-1)\*(C'\fR, so unless \f(CW\*(C`df\*(C'\fR is a whole number some
derivative of it is infinite at \f(CW\*(C`w = 0\*(C'\fR and Simpson\*(Aqs error bound does not
hold: two good digits at \f(CW\*(C`df = 1.2\*(C'\fR with \f(CW\*(C`sig_level = 1e\-4\*(C'\fR, five at
\&\f(CW\*(C`df = 1.2\*(C'\fR, nine at \f(CW\*(C`df = 1.8\*(C'\fR. Substituting \f(CW\*(C`w = z**m\*(C'\fR, with \f(CW\*(C`m\*(C'\fR chosen so
that \f(CW\*(C`m*df \- 1 >= 3\*(C'\fR, restores the bounded derivatives and brings all of
those to machine precision. It also puts the origin\*(Aqs contribution at zero,
which subsumes a separate bug: the sum had been dropping its \f(CW\*(C`u = 0\*(C'\fR endpoint
term, worth 7e\-7 of absolute power at \f(CW\*(C`df == 1\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`nu\*(C'\fR is now also floored the
way R floors it, per sample rather than in total.
.IP \(bu 4
The same density has standard deviation \f(CW\*(C`1/sqrt(2*df)\*(C'\fR and so narrows without
bound, while the grid did not. Past \f(CW\*(C`df\*(C'\fR of about 1e7 the steps went clean
over the peak: \f(CW\*(C`power_t_test(n => 4e7, delta => 0)\*(C'\fR returned 0.138 where the
answer can only be \f(CW\*(C`sig_level/2\*(C'\fR, and a large\-cohort \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR solved 9% low. Above
\&\f(CW\*(C`df\*(C'\fR of 1e3 the steps now go on \f(CW\*(C`w\*(C'\fR across +/\- 12 standard deviations of the
mode, with the chi normalisation taken from Stirling\*(Aqs series to keep the peak
height from cancelling away; and above 4e5, where those log terms cancel too
hard for any grid to help, the Abramowitz & Stegun 26.7.10 asymptotic form
takes over \-\- the same formula, at the same cut\-off, that R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`pnt.c\*(C'\fR uses.
That is also 25 times quicker than integrating.
.IP \(bu 4
The power was formed as \f(CW\*(C`1 \- P(T <= t)\*(C'\fR, which loses most of its digits to
cancellation when the power is small. It is now integrated as the upper tail
directly.
.IP \(bu 4
The four inverse solvers were plain bisection stopped at the bracket width,
which capped \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`delta\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`sig_level\*(C'\fR at R\*(Aqs own four or five
significant figures. They now use regula falsi with the Illinois correction
against a relative tolerance, so they match machine\-precision \f(CW\*(C`brentq\*(C'\fR roots
to ~1e\-13 in fewer evaluations than the bisection took. The \f(CW\*(C`tol\*(C'\fR default
moved from \f(CW1.22e\-4\fR to \f(CW1e\-12\fR to match.
.IP \(bu 4
Nothing checked that the bracket held a root, so an unreachable target came
back as a bracket endpoint wearing the requested power: solving for \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR with
\&\f(CW\*(C`power => 0.01\*(C'\fR returned \f(CW\*(C`delta * 1e7\*(C'\fR, and with a negative \f(CW\*(C`delta\*(C'\fR returned a
negative standard deviation. Unreachable targets now croak and name the range
searched. \f(CW\*(C`sig_level\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`power\*(C'\fR outside \f(CW\*(C`[0, 1]\*(C'\fR, an \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR below 2, a negative
\&\f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, and an unrecognised \f(CW\*(C`type\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fR are rejected as well \-\-
\&\f(CW\*(C`type => \*(Aqtwosample\*(Aq\*(C'\fR used to be read silently as \f(CW\*(Aqtwo.sample\*(Aq\fR.
.PP
New test file \f(CW\*(C`t/power_t_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR carries the cross\-validated grid.
