.IX Subsection "0.291 2026-08-04 CDT"
POD formatting improvements
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR\fI, \fR\f(CI\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "lm, glm"
.PP
Formula parsing and data reading are now shared between \f(CW\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR too, so the
two agree on what a formula means and on what a row is called. \f(CW\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR had the better
parser and \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR the better row naming; each now has both.
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR\fB now names rows the way \fR\f(CB\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fB does\fR — from a \f(CW\*(C`row.names\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`_row\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`rownames\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`.rownames\*(C'\fR column when the data has one, and 1\-based integers
otherwise. \f(CW\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR previously always used integers, so \f(CW\*(C`fitted.values\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`residuals\*(C'\fR came back keyed \f(CW\*(C`1..n\*(C'\fR for data whose rows had names, and did not
match what \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR returned for the same data; the \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR
documentation already described the shared behaviour. A row\-name column is a label
rather than a measurement, so \f(CW\*(C`y ~ .\*(C'\fR now excludes it in both.
.PP
Design\-matrix construction is now shared between \f(CW\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR, and decides a
categorical column\*(Aqs coding term by term using R\*(Aqs margin rule: the reference
level is dropped when the term with that column removed is itself in the model.
Three bugs fall out of that, all confirmed against R 4.6.1 and statsmodels
0.14.6.
.PP
Bug fixes
.IX Subsection "Bug fixes"
.PP
Four in \f(CW\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR, from the parser it now shares with \f(CW\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR. Three of them ended the
same way: a term that names no column evaluates to \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR for every row, every row
is dropped as incomplete, and the fit dies with \f(CW\*(C`0 degrees of freedom (too many
NAs or parameters > observations)\*(C'\fR — never mentioning the formula.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fB truncated a formula at 511 characters.\fR It copied the formula into a
fixed \f(CW\*(C`char[512]\*(C'\fR, so a model with enough predictors to overrun that lost the
tail. The buffer now grows with the formula.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fB did not understand \fR\f(CB\*(C`.\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR It parsed the formula before reading the data,
so there were no column names to expand \f(CW\*(C`.\*(C'\fR into and the term stayed a literal
\&\f(CW\*(C`.\*(C'\fR. Formula splitting now happens first and term expansion after the data is
read, so \f(CW\*(C`y ~ .\*(C'\fR works in both.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fB did not understand \fR\f(CB\*(C`+ 0\*(C'\fR\fB or a leading \fR\f(CB\*(C`0 +\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR Only \f(CW\*(C`\- 1\*(C'\fR suppressed the
intercept; the other two spellings R accepts left a term named \f(CW0\fR. All three now
work in both, as do \f(CW\*(C`+ 1\*(C'\fR and a leading \f(CW\*(C`1 +\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fB read the \fR\f(CB\-1\fR\fB inside \fR\f(CBI(...)\fR\fB as intercept suppression.\fR It searched the
whole right\-hand side for the substring, so \f(CW\*(C`y ~ I(x\-1)\*(C'\fR silently became
\&\f(CW\*(C`y ~ I(x) \- 1\*(C'\fR: a different model, fitted without complaint. The scan now steps
over \f(CWI(...)\fR, leaving the term alone. \f(CWI()\fR still supports only \f(CW\*(C`^power\*(C'\fR, so
that formula is an error in both rather than a wrong answer in one.
.PP
And the three that fall out of the shared design matrix:
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBA categorical column in a model with no intercept lost a level.\fR With no
intercept there is no baseline for a reference level to be measured against, so
R codes the factor in full — one column per group, each coefficient that
group\*(Aqs own mean. Both functions dropped the reference level anyway, so
\&\f(CW\*(C`len ~ supp \- 1\*(C'\fR fitted \f(CW\*(C`len ~ suppVC \- 1\*(C'\fR: a model forcing every observation
at the reference level to a fitted value of 0. On R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`ToothGrowth\*(C'\fR that meant
a residual sum of squares of 16056 against R\*(Aqs 3247, and an R² of 0.35 against
0.87. Where two categorical main effects appear with no intercept only the
first is coded in full, as in R, since coding both would be rank deficient.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBAn interaction involving a categorical column could not be built.\fR The
interaction was looked up as a single column literally named \f(CW\*(C`dose:supp\*(C'\fR;
finding none, it evaluated to \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR for every row, every row was dropped as
incomplete, and the fit died with \f(CW\*(C`0 degrees of freedom (too many NAs or
parameters > observations)\*(C'\fR. Interactions now expand to the product of their
components\*(Aq indicator columns, so \f(CW\*(C`len ~ dose * supp\*(C'\fR gives \f(CW\*(C`dose\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`suppVC\*(C'\fR
and \f(CW\*(C`dose:suppVC\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR already understood such coefficient names; now
they can be produced.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`a*b*c\*(C'\fR\fB expanded only its first \fR\f(CB\*(C`*\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR Crossing is associative, so
\&\f(CW\*(C`y ~ a * b * c\*(C'\fR now yields every non\-empty subset (\f(CW\*(C`a\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`b\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`c\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`a:b\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`a:c\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`b:c\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`a:b:c\*(C'\fR), ordered by degree as R\*(Aqs \f(CWterms()\fR orders them. Previously the
chunk was split once, producing the unusable terms \f(CW\*(C`b*c\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`a:b*c\*(C'\fR, and the
fit died the same way as above. Crossing more than 16 columns now croaks rather
than expanding to 2^n terms.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR\fB scored reference\-level rows as if the term were absent.\fR It
registered factor dummies from \f(CW\*(C`levels[1..]\*(C'\fR only, on the assumption that a
reference level never has a coefficient — true for a factor coded by contrasts,
but not for one coded in full. Every row at the reference level of a
no\-intercept model therefore came back 0.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fB halved its IRLS step whenever the deviance rose, costing iterations and
accuracy in the standard errors.\fR R truncates a step only when the deviance
comes out non\-finite; a deviance that merely increases is not divergence. The
standard IRLS start puts \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR at \f(CW\*(C`y + 0.1\*(C'\fR, essentially on the data, so the
initial deviance is near zero and the first real step almost always raises it —
on the nine\-point poisson fit in \f(CW\*(C`t/glm.t\*(C'\fR, from 0.016 to 1.54. That was read as
divergence and the step was halved ten times over, turning R\*(Aqs four iterations
into seven.
.Sp
The extra iterations reached the same coefficients, so the symptom appeared
only in the standard errors. They are built from the information matrix of the
\&\fIpenultimate\fR iterate — in R because \f(CW\*(C`summary.glm\*(C'\fR inverts the QR that
\&\f(CW\*(C`glm.fit\*(C'\fR kept from its last weighted least squares call, and here because the
IRLS sweep leaves that inverse in place — so stopping on a different iteration
than R means reporting a different matrix. Poisson standard errors were 5e\-8 to
2e\-5 away from R\*(Aqs while the coefficients agreed to twelve digits; they now
agree to about 1e\-14. Binomial standard errors were up to 6e\-7 out and now
agree to 2e\-14, except on a near\-separable fit, where the \f(CW\*(C`varmu\*(C'\fR floor of
1e\-10 (a guard against dividing by an underflowed variance) accounts for the
remaining difference — 1.9e\-9 on \f(CW\*(C`am ~ wt * hp\*(C'\fR, where three of 32 fitted
probabilities are within 1e\-12 of 0 or 1 and R itself warns. Gaussian fits are
unaffected: their weights are all 1, so the matrix is \f(CW\*(C`X\*(AqX\*(C'\fR either way.
.Sp
The same condition had its \f(CW\*(C`isfinite\*(C'\fR test on the accepting side, so a
genuinely divergent step producing a non\-finite deviance was kept rather than
truncated. That is now the one case that does trigger halving.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBThe negative\-binomial theta alternation stopped early and started from the
wrong place.\fR \f(CW\*(C`MASS::glm.nb\*(C'\fR does not simply maximise over theta; it alternates
between an IRLS fit at the current theta and a fresh ML estimate of theta at the
current fitted means, and which fit it lands on depends on the schedule. Four
details of that schedule were wrong here, and all four are now reproduced:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 4
The alternation stopped on a relative test of the log\-likelihood alone,
\&\f(CW\*(C`|dll| < 1e\-7 * (|ll| + 0.1)\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR requires
\&\f(CW\*(C`(|dLm| / d1 + |dtheta|) < 1e\-8\*(C'\fR with \f(CW\*(C`d1 = sqrt(2 * max(1, df.residual))\*(C'\fR
taken from its Poisson pass — theta itself has to have settled, not just the
log\-likelihood. The old test was satisfied roughly 2e\-5 of log\-likelihood
early, which left theta 8e\-7 out and dragged the coefficients 8e\-6 with it.
.IP \(bu 4
The first pass now runs as a genuine \fBPoisson\fR fit, as \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs does,
rather than a negative\-binomial fit at a large stand\-in theta. That pass
supplies both the first theta and the \f(CW\*(C`d1\*(C'\fR above.
.IP \(bu 4
Later passes are \fBwarm started\fR from the previous pass\*(Aqs means
(\f(CW\*(C`etastart = log(mu)\*(C'\fR), so they converge to the fit \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR reaches rather
than to the same optimum approached from a cold start.
.IP \(bu 4
Theta is re\-estimated at the means each pass \fBstarted\fR from, not the ones it
produced: \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR calls \f(CW\*(C`theta.ml(Y, mu)\*(C'\fR and only then reassigns
\&\f(CW\*(C`mu <\- fit$fitted.values\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`theta.ml\*(C'\fR itself now also uses MASS\*(Aqs own stopping
rule, an absolute Newton\-step tolerance of \f(CW\*(C`.Machine$double.eps^0.25\*(C'\fR.
.RE
.RS 4
.Sp
Across eighteen fits spanning dispersion from theta 0.41 to theta 69000, theta
now agrees with \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR to 3.4e\-9, coefficients to 5.8e\-9, standard errors to
8.4e\-10 and deviance to 1.3e\-9 — previously 8e\-7, 8e\-6, 3e\-6 and 6e\-7. The one
exception is genuinely near\-Poisson data, where theta is not identified at all
(its own standard error exceeds the estimate, and \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`theta.ml\*(C'\fR reports
"iteration limit reached"); theta there agrees only to about 4e\-6 relative,
while the coefficients still agree to 1.6e\-10.
.Sp
A separate consequence: a negative\-binomial fit with theta supplied was
starting from the Poisson \f(CW\*(C`mustart\*(C'\fR of \f(CW\*(C`y + 0.1\*(C'\fR, where R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`negative.binomial()$initialize\*(C'\fR sets \f(CW\*(C`y + (y == 0)/6\*(C'\fR. Different starting
values walk different iterates, and since the standard errors come from the
penultimate one, that showed as standard errors 6e\-7 from R\*(Aqs while the
coefficients agreed to 1e\-9. Such fits now match R to 2e\-15.
.Sp
Note on that comparison: standard errors for a negative\-binomial fit hold the
dispersion at 1, which is what \f(CW\*(C`glm.nb\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`summary.negbin\*(C'\fR do. R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`summary.glm\*(C'\fR, handed a \f(CW\*(C`negative.binomial\*(C'\fR family directly, instead \fIestimates\fR
the dispersion and prints standard errors scaled by its square root — 1.0839 on
one of the test data sets, so about 4% larger. Compare against
\&\f(CW\*(C`summary(fit, dispersion = 1)\*(C'\fR to see the values this module reports.
.RE
.SS "0.29 2026\-08\-03 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.29 2026-08-03 CDT"
\fIt_test\fR
.IX Subsection "t_test"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR was cross\-checked against R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`stats::t.test\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`scipy.stats\*(C'\fR case by
case, including the cases their own suites pin: R\*(Aqs regression tests
(\f(CW\*(C`reg\-tests\-1a.R\*(C'\fR, "t.test with one group of size one") and scipy\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`TestTTest_1samp\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`TestTTest_ind.test_special_cases\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`test_ttest_rel_ci_1d\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`test_1samp_ci_1d\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`test_pvalue_ci\*(C'\fR. On 2000 randomised comparisons against
R — all four modes, all three alternatives, random \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`conf_level\*(C'\fR, sample
sizes 2 to 40 and data scales spanning 1e\-4 to 1e4 — the statistic and the
degrees of freedom agree to 2e\-11 and the p\-value to 3e\-9, holding to eight
digits even where the p\-value is subnormal (5e\-310). What the comparison did
turn up was seven ways a call could come back wrong rather than loud, all of
them now fixed and covered by \f(CW\*(C`t/t_test.t\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR\fB was coerced to 0 instead of being dropped.\fR This is the one worth
re\-running results over. \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR did not filter missing values, so a column
with gaps in it was tested with every gap counted as a zero: R gives
\&\f(CW\*(C`t.test(c(1,2,NA,4,5))\*(C'\fR a \f(CW\*(C`t\*(C'\fR of 3.286 on 3 degrees of freedom, and \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR
answered 2.588 on 4. No error, no warning, and an answer close enough to the
real one to look right. \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR are now dropped the way R drops \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR,
per\-vector for a one\-sample or unpaired test, and on complete cases when
\&\f(CW\*(C`paired\*(C'\fR so a half\-missing pair goes whole rather than contributing a
difference against zero.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBA \fR\f(CB\*(C`y\*(C'\fR\fB of fewer than two observations returned a silent \fR\f(CB\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR \f(CW\*(C`var_y\*(C'\fR
divided by \f(CW\*(C`ny \- 1\*(C'\fR, so \f(CW\*(C`t_test(\e@x, [$one_value])\*(C'\fR propagated \f(CW\*(C`0/0\*(C'\fR into the
statistic, the p\-value and both interval bounds without raising. The two
thresholds R uses are now both in place: a Welch test needs a variance from
each side and refuses without one, while a pooled test tolerates a side of one
observation, since that side contributes no sum of squares. That second case is
what R\*(Aqs own regression suite pins — \f(CW\*(C`t.test(y=x[1], x=x[\-1], var.equal=TRUE)\*(C'\fR
is a well\-defined test with 8 degrees of freedom, and \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR now answers it
instead of returning \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR in one direction and croaking in the other. An empty
\&\f(CW\*(C`y\*(C'\fR is caught by the same check.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fR\fB was never validated.\fR The p\-value helper fell through to
two\-sided for any string it did not recognise, so a typo — \f(CW\*(Aqgerater\*(Aq\fR — ran a
different test than the caller asked for and reported nothing. It is now
checked the way R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`match.arg\*(C'\fR checks it. \f(CW\*(C`scipy\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs \f(CW"two\-sided"\fR spelling is
unambiguous, so it is accepted rather than rejected.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBA one\-sided interval was wrong when \fR\f(CB\*(C`conf_level < 0.5\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR That case needs a
negative t quantile, and \f(CW\*(C`qt_tail\*(C'\fR searched upward from zero only, so it
returned roughly zero and collapsed the bound onto \f(CW\*(C`mu\*(C'\fR: R puts the upper bound
of \f(CW\*(C`t.test(1:10, mu=5, conf.level=0.3, alternative="less")\*(C'\fR at 4.9797 where
\&\f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR reported 5.0000000036. \f(CW\*(C`qt_tail\*(C'\fR now reduces by symmetry first, so the
root it brackets is always positive.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`qt_tail\*(C'\fR\fB silently saturated at 1e6.\fR Past that its doubling loop gave up
and returned the ceiling, so \f(CW\*(C`conf_level\*(C'\fR of 0.99999999 and 0.9999999999 came
back with the \fIidentical\fR interval, ±1048576, against R\*(Aqs ±6.4e7 and ±6.4e9.
The ceiling is gone; the loop now runs until \f(CW\*(C`t * t\*(C'\fR would overflow.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBInterval accuracy no longer depends on the data\*(Aqs scale.\fR \f(CW\*(C`qt_tail\*(C'\fR
bisected to an absolute 1e\-8 on the quantile, which is 1e\-8 × \f(CW\*(C`std_err\*(C'\fR on the
interval — fine for data around 1, an error of 2 units for data around 1e9. It
now bisects to adjacent doubles. Worst interval error across the 2000
randomised cases went from 2.1e\-4 to 5.3e\-11 relative. At extreme
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf_level\*(C'\fR this makes \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR the more accurate of the two: \f(CW\*(C`t.test\*(C'\fR asks
for \f(CW\*(C`qt(1 \- alpha/2, df)\*(C'\fR, and representing a 5e\-9 tail as the double
\&\f(CW\*(C`1 \- 5e\-9\*(C'\fR costs eight significant figures of it, so R\*(Aqs own interval for
\&\f(CW\*(C`conf.level=0.99999999\*(C'\fR is off by 0.7 in the eighth digit. Working in the upper
tail throughout agrees with R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`qt(alpha/2, df, lower.tail=FALSE)\*(C'\fR to 15
digits.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fB"Essentially constant" was an absolute test.\fR Only an exactly\-zero variance
was rejected, so a spread below what a double can resolve at the data\*(Aqs own
magnitude was reported as a finding: four values around 1e10 differing by 1e\-5
gave \f(CW\*(C`t\*(C'\fR = 4e15 and a p\-value of 3e\-47. The comparison is now relative, as R\*(Aqs
is. The exactly\-zero case, where R returns \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR, raises here instead.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBA defined non\-array \fR\f(CB\*(C`y\*(C'\fR\fB was ignored.\fR \f(CW\*(C`t_test(\e@x, y => 5)\*(C'\fR quietly ran a
one\-sample test. It now raises. An explicit \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR still means absent, as R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`y = NULL\*(C'\fR does.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`qt_tail\*(C'\fR is shared with \f(CW\*(C`power_t_test\*(C'\fR, which gains the same precision; it is
only ever called there with a tail below 0.5, so nothing about its behaviour
changes. \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR remains allocation\-free — the missing\-value filtering happens
inside the same single Welford pass that was already there, and the result hash
is built after the last error check rather than before the first.
.PP
\fIwrite_table: \fR\f(CI\*(C`tex.longtable.head\*(C'\fR
.IX Subsection "write_table: tex.longtable.head"
.PP
A \f(CW\*(C`longtable\*(C'\fR freezes only the header sitting inside \f(CW\*(C`\eendfirsthead\*(C'\fR /
\&\f(CW\*(C`\eendhead\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`tex.longtable\*(C'\fR never wrote those blocks — its header was an
ordinary first body row, leaving the frozen one to be hand\-written by the
caller. That header then had no link to \f(CW\*(C`col.names\*(C'\fR: reorder the columns and
the labels at the top of every page keep the old order while the data below
them moves, and the generated header appears again as a duplicate first row.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`tex.longtable.head\*(C'\fR generates the repeat machinery from the table\*(Aqs own header
record, so it cannot drift. A true\-but\-numeric value emits
\&\f(CW\*(C`\eendfirsthead\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`\eendhead\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`\eendfoot\*(C'\fR with no continuation caption; any other
true value is the caption used on pages after the first, written verbatim.
Implies \f(CW\*(C`tex.longtable\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`tex.longtable\*(C'\fR on its own is unchanged.
.PP
The wrapper keeps one static token, the \f(CW\*(C`\ehline\*(C'\fR closing its \f(CW\*(C`\ecaption\*(C'\fR line,
because a leading \f(CW\*(C`\ehline\*(C'\fR in an \f(CW\*(C`\einput\*(C'\fRed file is a \f(CW\*(C`Misplaced \enoalign\*(C'\fR
error — TeX has already begun the row by the time it expands the \f(CW\*(C`\einput\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\fIskew, kurtosis\fR
.IX Subsection "skew, kurtosis"
.PP
Two new XS functions describing the shape of a sample beyond its spread:
\&\f(CW\*(C`skew\*(C'\fR for the third central moment and \f(CW\*(C`kurtosis\*(C'\fR for the fourth. Both take
arguments the way \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR do — numbers, array references or a mixture,
flattened into one sample — and both also accept \f(CW\*(C`x => \e@data\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`type => 1|2|3\*(C'\fR.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`type\*(C'\fR selects among the three sample conventions, which disagree noticeably on
small samples. The default is \f(CW\*(C`type => 2\*(C'\fR: \f(CW\*(C`G1\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`G2\*(C'\fR, the estimators
unbiased for a normal sample, as reported by SAS, SPSS, Stata, Excel\*(Aqs \f(CWSKEW()\fR
and \f(CWKURT()\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`scipy.stats\*(C'\fR with \f(CW\*(C`bias => FALSE\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`type => 1\*(C'\fR is the plain
moment ratio (\f(CW\*(C`moments::skewness\*(C'\fR) and \f(CW\*(C`type => 3\*(C'\fR is \f(CW\*(C`b1\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`b2\*(C'\fR
(\f(CW\*(C`e1071::skewness\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs own default). All three, for both functions, agree with R
to about 1e\-15. \f(CW\*(C`kurtosis\*(C'\fR returns \fIexcess\fR kurtosis — 3 is already subtracted,
so a normal sample sits near 0.
.IP \(bu 4
One pass, no allocation: the third and fourth central moments accumulate
through Welford\*(Aqs recurrence extended to higher moments (Terriberry) rather
than the textbook expansion in raw moments. That expansion is not usable on
real data — for a column of values around 1e7, a lab value in the wrong units
or a timestamp, \f(CW\*(C`sum(x**3)/n\*(C'\fR is about 1e21 while the third central moment is
single digits, so every significant figure cancels away.
.IP \(bu 4
A constant sample croaks rather than returning a silent \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR from \f(CW\*(C`0/0\*(C'\fR, and
a \f(CW\*(C`type\*(C'\fR whose denominator the sample is too small for (\f(CW\*(C`type => 2\*(C'\fR needs
\&\f(CW\*(C`n >= 3\*(C'\fR for \f(CW\*(C`skew\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`n >= 4\*(C'\fR for \f(CW\*(C`kurtosis\*(C'\fR) says which.
.IP \(bu 4
Both read tied arrays. \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR on a tied array returns a deferred \f(CW\*(C`PVLV\*(C'\fR
rather than the value, and \f(CW\*(C`SvOK\*(C'\fR on one of those is false until its
get\-magic has run, so without an \f(CW\*(C`SvGETMAGIC\*(C'\fR every element of a tied array
looks undefined.
.PP
\fImedian\fR
.IX Subsection "median"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR now reads tied arrays too. It already had a separate \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR path
for them — a tied array keeps nothing in \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR, so the fast path would read
off a null pointer — but that path was missing the \f(CW\*(C`SvGETMAGIC\*(C'\fR described
above, so it rejected every tied array as undefined instead of computing the
answer. \f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR still reject tied arrays.
They have no \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR fast path to guard, so they croak rather than crash, and
the same one\-line fix would make each of them work.
.PP
\fIoneway_test\fR
.IX Subsection "oneway_test"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR was cross\-checked case by case against R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`stats::oneway.test\*(C'\fR
(both branches), R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`anova(aov())\*(C'\fR for the \f(CW\*(C`Sum Sq\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`Mean Sq\*(C'\fR columns,
\&\f(CW\*(C`statsmodels.stats.oneway.anova_oneway(use_var="unequal")\*(C'\fR and
\&\f(CW\*(C`scipy.stats.f_oneway\*(C'\fR. The 37 data sets are R\*(Aqs own built\-ins — \f(CW\*(C`chickwts\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`InsectSprays\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`PlantGrowth\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`iris\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`ToothGrowth\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mtcars\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`warpbreaks\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`sleep\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`airquality\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`CO2\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`esoph\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`OrchardSprays\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`faithful\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`quakes\*(C'\fR —
plus hand\-built numerical edge cases. The statistic and both degrees of freedom
already matched R everywhere; what the comparison turned up was four ways a call
could come back wrong rather than loud, all now fixed and covered by
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/oneway_test.R.scipy.t\*(C'\fR. Statistic, degrees of freedom and p\-value now agree
with R to 1.3e\-12 relative error across all 37, and on 2000 randomised
comparisons against R — both branches, 2 to 8 groups, sizes 2 to 40,
deliberately heteroscedastic, data scales 1e\-4 to 1e4 — the statistic and the
degrees of freedom agree to 1e\-12 and the p\-value to 8e\-11, the worst of those
being a p\-value of 2.4e\-66.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBEvery p\-value below about 1e\-16 was returned as a flat 0.\fR \f(CWPr(>F)\fR was
built as \f(CW\*(C`1 \- pf(F, df1, df2)\*(C'\fR, and 1 minus something that close to 1 has no
bits left to carry the answer: \f(CW\*(C`faithful\*(C'\fR split at \f(CW\*(C`waiting > 70\*(C'\fR should give
\&\f(CW1.2099104551915e\-76\fR under Welch and \f(CW5.50783574504386e\-103\fR pooled, and
\&\f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR reported \f(CW0\fR for both. Anything from about 1e\-9 downward was
losing relative precision the same way, quietly — \f(CW\*(C`ToothGrowth\*(C'\fR by dose came
back as \f(CW9.99200722162641e\-16\fR against R\*(Aqs \f(CW9.53272701169993e\-16\fR, off by 4.6%
with nothing to indicate it. The p\-value is now evaluated in the upper tail
directly, via the beta symmetry \f(CW\*(C`1 \- I_x(a, b) = I_{1\-x}(b, a)\*(C'\fR, so no
subtraction from 1 happens at any point, and the range down to the smallest
representable double is reported at full precision.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBAn \fR\f(CB\*(C`F\*(C'\fR\fB of \fR\f(CB\*(C`Inf\*(C'\fR\fB produced a p\-value of \fR\f(CB\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR\fB instead of 0.\fR When every group
is constant but their means differ, the within\-group sum of squares is 0 and
\&\f(CW\*(C`F\*(C'\fR is legitimately infinite; R reports \f(CW\*(C`p = 0\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`pf\*(C'\fR formed
\&\f(CW\*(C`df1*f/(df1*f + df2)\*(C'\fR, which is \f(CW\*(C`Inf/Inf\*(C'\fR — a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR that propagated straight
into \f(CWPr(>F)\fR. \f(CW\*(C`Inf\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR are now handled explicitly, matching R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`p = 0\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`p = NaN\*(C'\fR respectively.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBA \fR\f(CB\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR\fB Welch denominator df was reported as 1e300.\fR A group with zero
variance gets an infinite Welch weight, which makes R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`tmp\*(C'\fR term \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR and
its denominator df \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR with it. \f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR had a \f(CW\*(C`(tmp > 0.0)\*(C'\fR guard that
a \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR fails, so it substituted a magic \f(CW1e300\fR — a number that reads as a
real, very large degrees of freedom and would be believed as one. The guard is
gone; \f(CW\*(C`Residuals\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`Df\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`Residuals\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`Mean Sq\*(C'\fR are \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR there, as in R.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`formula\*(C'\fR\fB mode read \fR\f(CB\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR\fB and non\-numeric response cells as 0.0.\fR The
hash and array\-of\-arrays shapes were already fixed to die on these (and pinned
by \f(CW\*(C`t/oneway_test.bugs.t\*(C'\fR), but the formula path has its own fill loop and was
missed, so
.PP
.Vb 2
\& oneway_test({ y => [1, 2, 3, undef, 5, 6], lab => [qw(a a a b b b)] },
\&     formula => \*(Aqy ~ lab\*(Aq);
\&
\&  silently tested group C<b> as C<(0, 5, 6)> — a mean of 3.67 instead of 5.5 — and
\&  returned an C<F> of 0.735 with no complaint. All three input shapes now enforce
\&  the documented contract identically.
.Ve
.PP
Two places where \f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR is the more accurate side and the reference is
not, now documented rather than treated as disagreements: the sums of squares
are accumulated two\-pass, so on two groups near 1e8 \f(CW\*(C`Residuals\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`Sum Sq\*(C'\fR is
exactly \f(CW10\fR where R\*(Aqs QR\-based \f(CW\*(C`anova(aov())\*(C'\fR gives \f(CW10.0000000521067\fR; and
where the exact between\-group sum of squares is 0, \f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR returns 0
rather than R\*(Aqs 1e\-30\-scale residue.
.SS "0.281 2026\-08\-03 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.281 2026-08-03 CDT"
\&\f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR (LikeR.xs) — the same answers in about an eighth of the time. On the
\&\f(CW\*(C`benchmark.pl\*(C'\fR case (10,000 normals in one array ref) a call went from 0.83 ms
to 0.097 ms, which puts it ahead of the two implementations it was behind:
\&\f(CW\*(C`numpy.median\*(C'\fR at 0.105 ms and R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR at 0.196 ms, measured on the same
machine. Small samples — a per\-group median under \f(CW\*(C`agg\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`group_by\*(C'\fR, which is
where most calls to it come from — went from 647 ns to 251 ns.
.PP
A median is the middle one or two values, so most of the sort the function used
to do was wasted work: \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR orders all n elements at a cost of n log n
comparisons, every one an indirect call through a function pointer the compiler
cannot see into, to answer a question that depends on one or two of them. Those
values are now selected instead, and the sample is walked once rather than
twice.
.IP \(bu 4
The selection is introselect, the same shape numpy\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`partition\*(C'\fR uses:
quickselect with a median\-of\-three pivot, an insertion sort once a range is
small, and a heapsort fallback past a depth limit, so an input crafted to
defeat the pivot choice degrades to O(n log n) rather than O(n²). The awkward
data people actually have comes out faster than random data rather than
slower — sorted, reversed, all\-equal and organ\-pipe samples of 100,000 values
each take about 0.25 ms against 0.90 ms for random ones. For an even count the
lower of the middle pair is the largest value left below the upper one, which
a scan of that side finds without a second selection.
.IP \(bu 4
The counting pass is gone. It walked every element through \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR before
any arithmetic, only to size the buffer; the array lengths give the same
count, exactly, because an undef anywhere still dies.
.IP \(bu 4
The pass that remains reads cells through \f(CW\*(C`AvARRAY\*(C'\fR instead of \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR,
with tied arrays kept on the \f(CW\*(C`av_fetch\*(C'\fR path, since only it sees their values.
.IP \(bu 4
A sample of 256 values or fewer is copied to the C stack rather than the heap,
so the common small call no longer pays for a malloc and free at all: 10,000
of them now grow RSS by nothing. Larger samples still copy n values, which is
what leaves the caller\*(Aqs array in its original order — the selection reorders
whatever it works on, and \f(CW\*(C`t/median.t\*(C'\fR checks that the input comes back
untouched.
.PP
Error messages that carry an index or a count were unreadable on older perls,
in nineteen places across LikeR.xs, and now are not. \f(CW\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR runs perl\*(Aqs own
formatter rather than the C library\*(Aqs, and that formatter does not understand
C99\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`z\*(C'\fR length modifier: it printed the conversion literally, so
\&\f(CW\*(C`median(1, undef)\*(C'\fR on perl 5.10 or 5.12 said \f(CWundefined value at argument index
%zu\fR instead of naming the argument that was undefined — the one thing the
message existed to say. \f(CW\*(C`min\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`mode\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sd\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`var\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`median\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`mcnemar_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`friedman_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`hoa2hoh\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR were all affected.
They now use \f(CW\*(C`UVuf\*(C'\fR, as the rest of the file already did. The \f(CW\*(C`snprintf\*(C'\fR calls
elsewhere in the file are unaffected and unchanged: those do go to the C
library, where \f(CW%zu\fR means what it says.
.PP
New \f(CW\*(C`t/croak.messages.t\*(C'\fR covers every one of those messages: each is triggered,
checked for the number it should name, and then swept for any conversion left
unexpanded, which is what will catch the next one written with \f(CW%zu\fR. Run
against the code as it stood before this change, thirty\-two of its assertions
fail on perl 5.10.
.PP
New \f(CW\*(C`t/median.t\*(C'\fR: every length from 1 to 25 and from 254 to 258 — either side of
the insertion\-sort cutoff and of the point where the buffer moves off the stack
— across sorted, reversed, all\-equal, two\-valued, duplicate\-heavy, organ\-pipe
and median\-of\-three\-killer samples, each checked against a plain Perl sort,
together with the error messages and \f(CW\*(C`Test::LeakTrace\*(C'\fR over the stack, heap,
mixed\-argument and croak paths.
.PP
fix for threaded Perls https://www.cpantesters.org/cpan/report/2dbacf8f\-7138\-1014\-a1ab\-f0f91cf3b922
