.IX Subsection "0.28 2026-08-02 CDT"
\&\f(CW\*(C`p_adjust\*(C'\fR (LikeR.xs) now takes a data frame as well as a flat list of
p\-values, and hands the corrected values back in the shape they arrived in. An
AoA, AoH, HoA or HoH goes in and a new frame of the same kind comes out, with
the same rows, columns and row labels; the input is left alone. Everything the
flat form did is unchanged — an arrayref of p\-values still returns a list, in
order, with the same numbers.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`columns => \*(Aqp_value\*(Aq\*(C'\fR (or an arrayref of names, or 0\-based positions for an
AoA) says which columns hold p\-values, and copies the rest of the frame
through untouched, so a results table with a \f(CW\*(C`gene\*(C'\fR column no longer has to
be taken apart and put back together around the call. Without \f(CW\*(C`columns\*(C'\fR
every cell is treated as a p\-value, which is right for a frame that is
nothing but p\-values; a label column in one dies with a message naming the
offending value and pointing at \f(CW\*(C`columns\*(C'\fR, rather than correcting a string
coerced to zero.
.IP \(bu 4
All the p\-values in the frame are corrected as one family, whichever shape
they came in, so the family size is the number of p\-value cells.
.IP \(bu 4
The method still reads positionally and may now also be given as
\&\f(CW\*(C`method => ...\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`none\*(C'\fR, which the function has always accepted, is now
documented along with the rest.
.IP \(bu 4
Cells are visited in a fixed order — by row and then column name, or column
name and then row for a HoA — so tied p\-values break the same way on every
run instead of following hash iteration order.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`drop_duplicates\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`t_test\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`vals\*(C'\fR: speed/RAM improvements
.PP
\&\fBIncompatible:\fR the \f(CW\*(Aq?\*(Aq\fR / \f(CW\*(Aqh\*(Aq\fR argument added in 0.27 is gone (lib/Stats/LikeR.pm). \f(CWagg(\*(Aqh\*(Aq)\fR, \f(CWread_table(\*(Aq?\*(Aq)\fR and the fifty\-odd other pure\-Perl functions that took it no longer print help and die — they treat the string as data, the way the XS functions always have. \f(CWh(\*(Aqagg\*(Aq)\fR, \f(CWh(*agg)\fR and \f(CWh(\e&agg)\fR are unchanged and remain the way to ask, for every function in the distribution.
\&\- It was a help route that only half the module had, so what a lone \f(CW\*(Aqh\*(Aq\fR meant depended on whether the callee happened to be written in XS or in Perl, and a column, file or option value really named \f(CW\*(Aqh\*(Aq\fR needed \f(CW\*(C`$Stats::LikeR::HELP = 0\*(C'\fR to get through. That variable is gone too; nothing reads its arguments for a help flag any more.
\&\- \f(CW\*(C`bedroc\*(C'\fR still prints its own short XS usage summary for \f(CW\*(C`bedroc(\*(Aqh\*(Aq | \*(AqH\*(Aq | \*(Aq?\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR, which is hand\-written and predates all of this.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`merge\*(C'\fR (LikeR.xs) — same joins, a third of the time and a fifth of the memory. Nothing about the result changes: every join type, shape combination and edge case produces exactly what it did before, and \f(CW\*(C`t/merge.t\*(C'\fR now checks all six input/output paths against a plain\-Perl reference join over a randomized corpus.
.PP
The old implementation transposed both frames into arrays of row hashes, joined those, and transposed the result back. A 10,000\-row HoA joined to itself therefore built 20,000 throwaway row hashes and copied every cell three times before returning. It now reads each frame where it lies — a HoA column by column, an AoH/HoH row by row — and writes the result straight into the shape being returned, so the only cells copied are the ones the caller keeps.
.IP \(bu 4
The right frame\*(Aqs index is a hash of row numbers chained through a flat array, rather than an array\-ref of index scalars per distinct key, and one reused buffer builds every join key instead of one scalar per row.
.IP \(bu 4
Column names are resolved to their column (HoA) or interned once as shared hash keys (AoH/HoH) before the join starts, so the per\-row work is a lookup rather than a lookup and a rehash.
.IP \(bu 4
Measured on the \f(CW\*(C`benchmark.pl\*(C'\fR case (two 10,000\-row frames, six columns, inner join on \f(CW\*(C`id\*(C'\fR): 0.052 s and 41.4 MB before, 0.017 s and 7.3 MB after. An outer join of the same frames went from 0.113 s to 0.008 s.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`write_table\*(C'\fR (LikeR.xs) — two changes, one of them incompatible.
\&\- Every format now prints the coloured \f(CW\*(C`wrote <file>\*(C'\fR confirmation line, not just LaTeX and \f(CW\*(C`.xlsx\*(C'\fR. Delimited output (csv/tsv) was silent before. The line is identical in all cases: the file name in black on cyan, with the SGR codes inline so there is still no \f(CW\*(C`Term::ANSIColor\*(C'\fR dependency. Nothing is announced when nothing is written.
\&\- \fBIncompatible:\fR \f(CW\*(C`row.names\*(C'\fR now defaults to \fBoff\fR in every format. It previously defaulted \fBon\fR everywhere, following R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`write.table\*(C'\fR, which meant a call that said nothing about row names got a label column and a leading empty header cell (\f(CW\*(C`,gene,n\*(C'\fR) it had not asked for. Pass \f(CW\*(C`row.names => 1\*(C'\fR for the old behaviour; \f(CW\*(C`row.names => \*(Aqcol\*(Aq\*(C'\fR is unchanged.
.PP
New \f(CW\*(C`h2aoh\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`aoh2h\*(C'\fR (lib/Stats/LikeR.pm), which add the flat hash to the shapes the conversion family understands. A plain hash is a two\-column table folded shut, and until now nothing would unfold it: \f(CW\*(C`value_counts\*(C'\fR hands one back, and no frame function would take it.
\&\- \f(CW\*(C`h2aoh(\e%h, var_name => .., value_name => ..)\*(C'\fR unfolds a flat hash into a two\-column AoH, one row per pair, under column names the caller picks. \f(CW\*(C`sort => \*(Aqkey\*(Aq | \*(Aqvalue\*(Aq | \*(Aqnone\*(Aq\*(C'\fR fixes the row order, which hash iteration otherwise leaves to chance; \f(CW\*(Aqvalue\*(Aq\fR is biggest\-first for numbers, so \f(CW\*(C`value_counts\*(C'\fR output comes out the way pandas\*(Aq \f(CW\*(C`Series.value_counts()\*(C'\fR orders it.
\&\- \f(CW\*(C`aoh2h\*(C'\fR folds a two\-column AoH back down, with \f(CW\*(C`duplicates => \*(Aqdie\*(Aq | \*(Aqfirst\*(Aq | \*(Aqlast\*(Aq\*(C'\fR deciding what a repeated key means. The two are exact inverses under their defaults.
\&\- The column options are named \f(CW\*(C`var_name\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`value_name\*(C'\fR after \f(CW\*(C`melt\*(C'\fR, which emits the same two columns. R spells this pair \f(CWtibble::enframe()\fR / \f(CWdeframe()\fR; pandas spells it \f(CW\*(C`pd.Series(d).reset_index()\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`Series.to_dict()\*(C'\fR.
