.IX Subsection "assign"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`assign\*(C'\fR now accepts a third kind of column value, \f(CW\*(C`map_cell { ... }\*(C'\fR, for editing an existing column in place — no "copy, substitute, return" boilerplate and no dependence on \f(CW\*(C`s///r\*(C'\fR (unavailable on the older perls this module supports).
.IP \(bu 4
Inside a \f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR block, \f(CW$_\fR is the \fBnamed column\*(Aqs current cell\fR (not the whole row), the block\*(Aqs return value is \fBignored\fR, and the modified \f(CW$_\fR is stored back: \f(CW\*(C`assign($df, \*(AqRes.\*(Aq => map_cell { s/^[A\-Z]:// })\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
The row is still available as \f(CW$_[0]\fR (sibling columns), the index as \f(CW$_[1]\fR, and the row key as \f(CW$_[2]\fR (HoH only).
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBUndef/missing cells pass through untouched\fR (undef in → undef out): the block is skipped for them, so \f(CW\*(C`s///\*(C'\fR never warns on an uninitialized value.
.IP \(bu 4
Supported on all three shapes; for HoA the target column must already exist. A plain \f(CW\*(C`sub { ... }\*(C'\fR is unchanged, so \f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR is purely additive.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`map_cell\*(C'\fR is exported alongside \f(CW\*(C`assign\*(C'\fR.
.PP
