Bug fixes
.IX Subsection "Bug fixes"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR\fB underflow on empty arrays.\fR Three loops were bounded by \f(CWav_len(...)\fR
compared against an unsigned counter; \f(CW\*(C`av_len\*(C'\fR returns \f(CW\-1\fR for an empty array,
which turned \f(CW\*(C`k <= len\*(C'\fR into a \f(CW\*(C`SIZE_MAX\*(C'\fR loop. The \f(CWstack()\fR value loop, the \f(CW\*(C`.\*(C'\fR
column\-expansion loop, and the \f(CW\*(C`group_stats\*(C'\fR column loop now use a signed
\&\f(CW\*(C`SSize_t\*(C'\fR bound.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBHoH row count.\fR Row count for hash\-of\-hashes input was taken from the return
value of \f(CW\*(C`hv_iterinit\*(C'\fR; it now uses \f(CWHvUSEDKEYS(hv)\fR with a separate
\&\f(CW\*(C`hv_iterinit\*(C'\fR, matching \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBBuffer overflow in interaction parsing.\fR \f(CW\*(C`strcpy(right, colon + 1)\*(C'\fR into a
fixed \f(CW\*(C`char right[256]\*(C'\fR is now \f(CW\*(C`snprintf(right, sizeof(right), ...)\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Performance / memory
.IX Subsection "Performance / memory"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBRemoved the per\-row \fR\f(CB\*(C`row_x\*(C'\fR\fB scratch allocation.\fR Design rows are built
directly into \f(CW\*(C`X_mat[valid_n]\*(C'\fR; \f(CW\*(C`valid_n\*(C'\fR simply does not advance on a rejected
row. Interaction columns read their operands from the same in\-progress row, so
the logic is unchanged.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`row_names\*(C'\fR\fB is no longer dead.\fR Surviving row names are transferred (pointer
move, no copy) into \f(CW\*(C`surv_names\*(C'\fR to key \f(CW\*(C`fitted.values\*(C'\fR; rejected rows are freed
in place.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBDropped a \fR\f(CB\*(C`restrict\*(C'\fR\fB UB.\fR \f(CW\*(C`orig_data_sv\*(C'\fR aliases \f(CW\*(C`data_sv\*(C'\fR; the \f(CW\*(C`restrict\*(C'\fR
qualifier was removed.
.PP
New, \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR\-compatible output keys
.IX Subsection "New, predict-compatible output keys"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`coefficients\*(C'\fR\fB\fR — OLS estimates recovered by back\-substitution on the R factor
left in \f(CW\*(C`X_mat\*(C'\fR against Q\*(Aqy in \f(CW\*(C`Y\*(C'\fR (no re\-derivation). Keys are the expanded term
names (\f(CW\*(C`Intercept\*(C'\fR, continuous names, \f(CW\*(C`base.level\*(C'\fR dummies, and \f(CW\*(C`a:b\*(C'\fR interaction
products). Aliased columns are reported as \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR, which \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR drops.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`fitted.values\*(C'\fR\fB\fR — \f(CW\*(C`Xb\*(C'\fR over the non\-aliased columns, keyed by surviving row
name. Computed from a snapshot of the design (\f(CW\*(C`Dsav\*(C'\fR) taken before the QR
overwrites \f(CW\*(C`X_mat\*(C'\fR. Costs one transient copy of the design matrix; negligible for
typical ANOVA where the column count is small.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`xlevels\*(C'\fR\fB\fR — sorted level list per factor, index 0 = reference, aligned with
the contrast coding used to build the dummies.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`family\*(C'\fR\fB\fR — \f(CW"gaussian"\fR.
.PP
Cleanup\-path correctness
.IX Subsection "Cleanup-path correctness"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`xlevels_hv\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`Dsav\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`surv_names\*(C'\fR are freed on both the "0 degrees of
freedom" croak and the normal exit. The interaction\-main\-effects croak in
PHASE 3 also frees \f(CW\*(C`xlevels_hv\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Known limitations (unchanged)
.IX Subsection "Known limitations (unchanged)"
.IP \(bu 4
The intercept\-stripping string surgery (\f(CW\-1\fR, \f(CW+0\fR, \f(CW+1\fR, ...) operates on the
whole RHS and can still mangle \f(CWI(x\-1)\fR\-style transforms; treat \f(CWI()\fR with
arithmetic constants carefully.
.IP \(bu 4
Top\-level keys \f(CW\*(C`coefficients\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`fitted.values\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`xlevels\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`family\*(C'\fR /
\&\f(CW\*(C`group_stats\*(C'\fR share the return hash with the ANOVA rows; a predictor literally
named one of those would collide.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "predict"
.PP
New: factor\-bearing interaction terms
.IX Subsection "New: factor-bearing interaction terms"
.PP
Previously, interaction coefficients such as \f(CW\*(C`GroupB:Sexmale\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`GroupB:x\*(C'\fR fell
through to the continuous \f(CW\*(C`evaluate_term\*(C'\fR path and died on a nonexistent column.
They are now handled directly:
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`dummy_hv\*(C'\fR\fB\fR stores each dummy\*(Aqs factor base index (an \f(CW\*(C`IV\*(C'\fR) instead of
\&\f(CW&PL_sv_yes\fR, so a dummy name maps back to its \f(CW\*(C`(base, level)\*(C'\fR in O(1)
(\f(CW\*(C`level == name + strlen(base)\*(C'\fR). \f(CW\*(C`hv_exists\*(C'\fR lookups are unaffected.
.IP \(bu 4
During coefficient caching, any \f(CW\*(C`:\*(C'\fR term with at least one factor\-dummy component
is routed to a separate list (\f(CW\*(C`icopy\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`ibeta\*(C'\fR); pure\-continuous interactions
(e.g. \f(CW\*(C`x:z\*(C'\fR) stay on the existing \f(CW\*(C`evaluate_term\*(C'\fR path, so prior behavior is
preserved.
.IP \(bu 4
Each routed term is parsed once into flat component arrays. Factor components
store a base index and level pointer; continuous components store the term string
and get the same up\-front column\-existence validation as main terms.
.IP \(bu 4
Per row, each factor\*(Aqs raw level is read once into \f(CW\*(C`raw_lv[]\*(C'\fR and reused by both
main effects and interactions (no duplicate \f(CW\*(C`get_data_string_alloc\*(C'\fR). An
interaction\*(Aqs value is the product of its components: a factor component
contributes \f(CW1.0\fR iff the row\*(Aqs level matches the dummy\*(Aqs level (reference levels
give \f(CW0\fR), continuous components go through \f(CW\*(C`evaluate_term\*(C'\fR.
.PP
This covers factor×factor, factor×continuous, continuous×continuous, and n\-way
combinations.
.PP
Other
.IX Subsection "Other"
.IP \(bu 4
HoH row count uses \f(CW\*(C`HvUSEDKEYS\*(C'\fR (already present).
.IP \(bu 4
The unseen\-factor\-level croak now frees every level string already read for the
current row, not just the current one.
.PP
\fITests\fR
.IX Subsection "Tests"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`aov.t\*(C'\fR\fB\fR — one\-way ANOVA against hand\-computed values (Df / Sum Sq / Mean Sq /
F / decomposition); identical results across HoA / HoH / AoH / stacked input;
simple regression; \f(CW\*(C`.\*(C'\fR expansion; intercept removal (\f(CW\-1\fR); two\-way with
interaction (Type I SS on a balanced design); NaN listwise deletion; all croak
paths; leak checks.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`predict.t\*(C'\fR\fB\fR — \f(CW\*(C`predict(training) == fitted.values\*(C'\fR round\-trips for one\-way,
regression, factor×factor, factor×continuous, and continuous×continuous models;
explicit predicted values; agreement across HoA / AoH / HoH / flat newdata;
no\-newdata path; binomial \f(CW\*(C`link\*(C'\fR vs \f(CW\*(C`response\*(C'\fR; gaussian identity link; all croak
paths; leak checks.
.PP
Leak tests use \f(CW\*(C`no_leaks_ok\*(C'\fR guarded by \f(CW\*(C`unless $INC{\*(AqDevel/Cover.pm\*(Aq}\*(C'\fR and skipped
when \f(CW\*(C`Test::LeakTrace\*(C'\fR is absent.
.PP
Assumptions worth confirming
.IX Subsection "Assumptions worth confirming"
.IP \(bu 4
The NaN\-deletion test relies on \f(CW\*(C`evaluate_term\*(C'\fR returning \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR for a non\-finite
response value (an \f(CW\*(C`Inf \- Inf\*(C'\fR NaN is fed in deterministically).
.IP \(bu 4
The continuous×continuous round\-trip relies on \f(CWevaluate_term("x:z")\fR yielding
\&\f(CW\*(C`x * z\*(C'\fR — the same assumption the pre\-existing \f(CW\*(C`predict\*(C'\fR continuous\-interaction
path already made. If that path was untested, this round\-trip now exercises it.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`view\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "view"
.PP
now returns colored output; fixed bug with incorrect widths; undefined values show as \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR rather than \f(CW\*(C`NA\*(C'\fR, as in Data::Printer
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`csort\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "csort"
.PP
now accepts Hash of Hashes; addition of \f(CW\*(C`restrict\*(C'\fR which should decrease calculation time
.PP
\fIfilter\fR
.IX Subsection "filter"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBAdded hash\-of\-hashes (HoH) input.\fR In addition to AoH and HoA, \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR now accepts an HoH (\f(CW\*(C`{ key => { col => val, ... }, ... }\*(C'\fR); each inner hash is one row, and matching keys are preserved by default (HoH \-> HoH).
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBAdded \fR\f(CB\*(C`output.type\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR \f(CW\*(C`filter($df, $pred, \*(Aqoutput.type\*(Aq => \*(Aqaoh\*(Aq|\*(Aqhoa\*(Aq)\*(C'\fR selects the returned shape (aliases \f(CW\*(C`out\*(C'\fR / \f(CW\*(C`output_type\*(C'\fR; a bare positional type also works). When omitted, the input shape is preserved. \f(CW\*(C`hoh\*(C'\fR is not a selectable output, since it would require choosing a key column.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CBcol()\fR\fB reworked, not removed.\fR Both predicate forms are kept: \f(CW\*(C`col(\*(Aqage\*(Aq) >= 18\*(C'\fR still works and is the concise/composable option, while a coderef covers everything else. Internally \f(CWcol()\fR is now \fBpure Perl\fR — an overloaded class that builds a per\-row closure — and \f(CW\*(C`filter\*(C'\fR unwraps that closure so \f(CWcol()\fR and a coderef share one evaluation path. The previous standalone XS predicate evaluator (\f(CW\*(C`filt_eval\*(C'\fR/\f(CW\*(C`filt_ctx\*(C'\fR) is gone; delete it if your tree still has it. One consequence: a \f(CWcol()\fR comparison now costs the same per row as the equivalent coderef (a Perl call), rather than being evaluated in C.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBUnchanged guarantees:\fR the input frame is never modified; \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR (and, for numeric ops, non\-numeric) cells never match a \f(CWcol()\fR comparison; AoH/HoH rows are shared rather than copied where possible; keep\-all/keep\-none shapes are well defined per output type; Perl 5.10 compatibility is retained. A latent \f(CWSvTRUE(POPs)\fR double\-evaluation in the per\-row call helper (which crashed on perls where \f(CW\*(C`SvTRUE\*(C'\fR is a multi\-eval macro) was fixed along the way.
.PP
\fIread_table\fR
.IX Subsection "read_table"
.PP
Added an opt\-in \f(CW\*(C`auto.row.names\*(C'\fR argument so \f(CW\*(C`read_table\*(C'\fR can read the file R
produces by default from \f(CW\*(C`write.table(x, sep="\et")\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The problem
.IX Subsection "The problem"
.PP
R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`write.table\*(C'\fR defaults to \f(CW\*(C`row.names=TRUE, col.names=TRUE\*(C'\fR, which writes the
row\-names column in every data row but emits \fBno header label for it\fR. So a
frame with N columns comes out as N header fields over N+1 data fields — e.g.
\&\f(CW\*(C`mtcars\*(C'\fR gives 11 headers but 12\-field rows. By default \f(CW\*(C`read_table\*(C'\fR (correctly)
rejects that as ragged:
.PP
.Vb 1
\& Alignment error on mtcars.tsv data row 1 (12 fields vs 11 headers).
.Ve
.PP
The change
.IX Subsection "The change"
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`auto.row.names\*(C'\fR turns on R\*(Aqs own \f(CW\*(C`read.table\*(C'\fR rule: \fBwhen, and only when, the
header is exactly one field short of the data rows, treat the first field of
each row as an (unlabelled) row\-names column.\fR
.PP
.Vb 2
\& # default: the leading column is named \*(Aqrow_name\*(Aq
\& my $df = read_table(\*(Aqmtcars.tsv\*(Aq, \*(Aqauto.row.names\*(Aq => 1);
\&
\& # or give it a name
\& my $df = read_table(\*(Aqmtcars.tsv\*(Aq, \*(Aqauto.row.names\*(Aq => \*(Aqmodel\*(Aq);
.Ve
.PP
The synthesized column behaves like any other first column: it appears in \f(CW\*(C`aoh\*(C'\fR
and \f(CW\*(C`hoa\*(C'\fR output, and for \f(CW\*(C`hoh\*(C'\fR it becomes the default key (so rows are keyed by
the model name). This also lines up with the existing handling of R\*(Aqs
\&\f(CW\*(C`col.names=NA\*(C'\fR output (a blank leading header), which still produces a
\&\f(CW\*(C`row_name\*(C'\fR column with no flag needed.
.PP
What did not change
.IX Subsection "What did not change"
.PP
The strict alignment check is still the default. Without \f(CW\*(C`auto.row.names\*(C'\fR the
lopsided file still croaks, and even with it, a row that is off by anything
other than exactly one field still croaks — so the corruption guard only relaxes
for the one case R itself treats specially.
.PP
Tested in \f(CW\*(C`t/read_table.2.t\*(C'\fR (16 assertions, Perl 5.10.1 and 5.38): aoh / hoa /
hoh output, custom column name, the already\-aligned file (flag is a no\-op), the
\&\f(CW\*(C`col.names=NA\*(C'\fR path, and the strict / ragged croak paths.
.PP
additional bugfix
.IX Subsection "additional bugfix"
.PP
.Vb 5
\& # This is a comment
\& id,name,val
\& 1,Alice,10.5
\& 2,Bob,
\& 3,Charlie,15.2
.Ve
.PP
would not be read correctly using \f(CW\*(C`read_table\*(C'\fR, but now is read correctly
.PP
\fIvalue_counts\fR
.IX Subsection "value_counts"
.PP
now accepts array of hashes
.SS "0.16  2026\-06\-17 CDT"
.IX Subsection "0.16 2026-06-17 CDT"
changes to dist.ini, the minimum Perl version disappeared when I fixed other problems
.PP
clarifications between run time and test dependencies
.PP
addition of \f(CW\*(C`csort\*(C'\fR function to sort AoH and HoA
.PP
addition of \f(CW\*(C`aoh2hoa\*(C'\fR to translate array of hashes into a hash of arrays
.PP
fix of long double functions: https://www.cpantesters.org/cpan/report/5d5d9836\-6a5f\-11f1\-aadb\-63fd6d8775ea
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`glm\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "glm"
.PP
output residual keys now use names, not integers
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`lm\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "lm"
.PP
\fIBug fixes\fR
.IX Subsection "Bug fixes"
.PP
\&\fBMemory leak on the zero\-degrees\-of\-freedom error path.\fR When
\&\f(CW\*(C`valid_n <= p\*(C'\fR, the cleanup freed the \f(CW\*(C`valid_row_names\*(C'\fR \fIarray\fR but not the
per\-row name strings it held (those had been transferred out of \f(CW\*(C`row_names\*(C'\fR,
whose own array was already freed). The strings leaked on every such error.
Added the per\-entry \f(CW\*(C`Safefree\*(C'\fR loop before freeing the array, matching the
normal path.
.PP
\&\fBHoH input validated only the first row.\fR Only the first hash value was
checked to be a \f(CW\*(C`HASHREF\*(C'\fR; subsequent values were \f(CW\*(C`SvRV\*(C'\fR\*(Aqd unconditionally, so
a malformed row (\f(CW\*(C`{ a => {...}, b => 5 }\*(C'\fR) dereferenced a non\-reference. Every
row is now validated, with the partial allocations cleaned up before the
\&\f(CW\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR, mirroring the existing AoH path.
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`isspace\*(C'\fR\fB on a possibly\-signed \fR\f(CB\*(C`char\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR \f(CWisspace(*src)\fR is undefined for
byte values ≥ 0x80 on platforms where \f(CW\*(C`char\*(C'\fR is signed. Cast to
\&\f(CW\*(C`(unsigned char)\*(C'\fR before the call.
.PP
\fISpeed / RAM improvements\fR
.IX Subsection "Speed / RAM improvements"
.PP
\&\fBFormula buffer is now heap\-allocated to fit.\fR \f(CW\*(C`char f_cpy[512]\*(C'\fR silently
truncated any longer formula. Replaced with a buffer sized to
\&\f(CW\*(C`strlen(formula) + 1\*(C'\fR, so there is no fixed limit and no truncation.
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`.\*(C'\fR\fB\-expansion buffer is now a growable heap buffer.\fR \f(CW\*(C`char rhs_expanded[2048]\*(C'\fR
silently dropped expanded terms once full. It is now a buffer that doubles on
demand. Appends also went from \f(CW\*(C`strcat\*(C'\fR (which rescans from the start every
time — O(n²) over many columns) to an O(1) amortised append that tracks the
write position.
.PP
\&\fBNo more per\-row scratch allocation in matrix construction.\fR The original
\&\f(CW\*(C`safemalloc\*(C'\fR\*(Aqd a \f(CW\*(C`row_x\*(C'\fR buffer, filled it, copied it into \f(CW\*(C`X\*(C'\fR, and freed it
\&\fIfor every row\fR — \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR allocations plus \f(CW\*(C`n*p\*(C'\fR copies. Each candidate row is now
written straight into \f(CW\*(C`X\*(C'\fR at its prospective commit slot; a row that fails
listwise deletion is simply overwritten by the next candidate. This removes the
\&\f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR allocate/free cycles and the copy loop entirely.
.PP
\&\fBCategorical levels sorted with \fR\f(CB\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR The level list used an O(n²) bubble
sort; replaced with \f(CW\*(C`qsort\*(C'\fR (relevant only for high\-cardinality factors).
.PP
\&\fBUnused tail of \fR\f(CB\*(C`X\*(C'\fR\fB reclaimed after listwise deletion.\fR \f(CW\*(C`X\*(C'\fR is allocated for
all \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR rows up front (\f(CW\*(C`valid_n\*(C'\fR is unknown until rows are scanned). When rows
are dropped, \f(CW\*(C`X\*(C'\fR is now \f(CW\*(C`Renew\*(C'\fRed down to \f(CW\*(C`valid_n * p\*(C'\fR, returning the unused
tail to the allocator before the OLS phase.
.PP
\&\fBMinor robustness.\fR The argument\-parsing index was widened from
\&\f(CW\*(C`unsigned short\*(C'\fR to \f(CW\*(C`I32\*(C'\fR to match \f(CW\*(C`items\*(C'\fR, and the HoH row count now uses
\&\f(CW\*(C`HvUSEDKEYS\*(C'\fR rather than relying on \f(CW\*(C`hv_iterinit\*(C'\fR\*(Aqs return value.
.PP
\fIKnown limitations (left unchanged)\fR
.IX Subsection "Known limitations (left unchanged)"
.IP \(bu 4
A multi\-way term such as \f(CW\*(C`a*b*c\*(C'\fR is split only on the first \f(CW\*(C`*\*(C'\fR, so it yields
\&\f(CW\*(C`a\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`b*c\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`a:b*c\*(C'\fR rather than a full three\-way expansion. Deeper
interactions silently fail (the unparsable term evaluates to \f(CW\*(C`NaN\*(C'\fR and the
rows are dropped). This matches the documented two\-way \f(CW\*(C`*\*(C'\fR support.
.IP \(bu 4
HoA input takes the row count from the first column; columns shorter than
that simply contribute dropped rows rather than raising an error.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`oneway_test\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "oneway_test"
.PP
Bug fixes
.IX Subsection "Bug fixes"
.PP
\&\fBMemory leaks on error paths.\fR Nearly every \f(CW\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR after an allocation
leaked memory. \f(CW\*(C`croak\*(C'\fR does a \f(CW\*(C`longjmp\*(C'\fR, so anything allocated but not yet
freed is lost. Affected paths:
.IP \(bu 4
AoA and hash first\-pass errors leaked \f(CW\*(C`sizes\*(C'\fR and any \f(CW\*(C`gnames[]\*(C'\fR entries
allocated so far.
.IP \(bu 4
Formula\-mode "not found as an array ref" errors leaked \f(CW\*(C`lhs\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`rhs\*(C'\fR.
.PP
All post\-allocation errors now route through a single \f(CW\*(C`fail:\*(C'\fR label that frees
every pointer unconditionally. Pointers are initialised to \f(CW\*(C`NULL\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`gnames\*(C'\fR
is zero\-allocated with \f(CW\*(C`Newxz\*(C'\fR, so the cleanup is always safe to run.
.PP
\&\fBUndefined and non\-numeric cells silently coerced to \fR\f(CB0.0\fR\fB.\fR The original
second pass used \f(CW\*(C`(svp && *svp) ? SvNV(*svp) : 0.0\*(C'\fR, meaning an \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR or
non\-numeric cell was quietly treated as zero, silently corrupting the
F\-statistic. Each cell is now validated with \f(CW\*(C`SvOK\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`looks_like_number\*(C'\fR;
the call dies naming the group and observation index, consistent with the rest
of \f(CW\*(C`Stats::LikeR\*(C'\fR (\f(CW\*(C`mean\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`sum\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cor\*(C'\fR, etc.).
.PP
\&\fBUnsigned wraparound on empty array input.\fR \f(CW\*(C`k = (size_t)av_len(in_av) + 1\*(C'\fR
cast to \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR \fIbefore\fR adding, so an empty array (\f(CW\*(C`av_len\*(C'\fR returns \f(CW\-1\fR)
produced \f(CW\*(C`SIZE_MAX\*(C'\fR rather than \f(CW0\fR. Changed to
\&\f(CW\*(C`k = (size_t)(av_len(in_av) + 1)\*(C'\fR so the \f(CW+1\fR is done in signed arithmetic
before the cast.
.PP
\&\fBUnreliable group count from \fR\f(CB\*(C`hv_iterinit\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR \f(CW\*(C`hv_iterinit\*(C'\fR returns the
number of buckets in use rather than the number of keys for tied hashes.
Replaced with \f(CW\*(C`HvUSEDKEYS\*(C'\fR, which always returns the correct key count.
.PP
Improvements
.IX Subsection "Improvements"
.PP
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`var.equal\*(C'\fR\fB accepted as an alias for \fR\f(CB\*(C`var_equal\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR R users write
\&\f(CW\*(C`var.equal\*(C'\fR; the argument parser now accepts both spellings.
.PP
\&\fBPerl memory API used throughout.\fR \f(CW\*(C`safemalloc\*(C'\fR and manual \f(CW\*(C`memcpy\*(C'\fR replaced
with \f(CW\*(C`Newx\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`Newxz\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`savepv\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`savepvn\*(C'\fR. \f(CW\*(C`savepvn\*(C'\fR additionally
preserves embedded NUL bytes in group key strings, which the previous
\&\f(CW\*(C`strlen\*(C'\fR\-based copies silently truncated.
.PP
Known limitations (not changed)
.IX Subsection "Known limitations (not changed)"
.IP \(bu 4
A factor column named \f(CW\*(C`Residuals\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`group_stats\*(C'\fR in a formula call will
collide with reserved top\-level keys in the result hash.
.IP \(bu 4
Group names containing an embedded NUL are stored correctly but are still
truncated at \f(CW\*(C`strlen\*(C'\fR when written into the output hash keys.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`view\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "view"
.PP
default view shifted to 80 characters to match Linux window length
.PP
New features
.IX Subsection "New features"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`rows\*(C'\fR\fB is accepted as a synonym for \fR\f(CB\*(C`n\*(C'\fR\fB\fR (the number of rows shown).
Passing both \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`rows\*(C'\fR is an error.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBUnknown arguments are now rejected.\fR \f(CW\*(C`view\*(C'\fR validates its argument names
against the documented set (\f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`rows\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`na\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max_width\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`ellipsis\*(C'\fR,
\&\f(CW\*(C`gap\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`cols\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`columns\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`to\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`return_only\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`row.names\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`row_names\*(C'\fR) and
dies listing any it does not recognise, so a misspelt option (e.g. \f(CW\*(C`widht\*(C'\fR)
is caught instead of silently ignored.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`n\*(C'\fR\fB / \fR\f(CB\*(C`rows\*(C'\fR\fB is validated.\fR It must be a non\-negative integer; \f(CW\*(C`undef\*(C'\fR or
a non\-numeric value now dies with a clear message instead of producing
warnings and being treated as \f(CW0\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBflat/simple hashes are accepted as input\fR
.PP
Bug fixes
.IX Subsection "Bug fixes"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`n => 0\*(C'\fR\fB now still prints the column header.\fR Column names were collected
only from the rows being shown, so requesting zero rows produced an empty
header line. At least one row is now scanned (when data exists) so the
header always lists the columns.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBAn empty hash (\fR\f(CB\*(C`{}\*(C'\fR\fB) no longer dies.\fR It was rejected as
\&\fI"neither ARRAY nor HASH"\fR; it is now shown as an empty table
(\f(CW\*(C`0 rows x 0 cols\*(C'\fR), matching the handling of an empty array.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBThe \fR\f(CB\*(C`row_names\*(C'\fR\fB alias now drives the Hash\-of\-Hashes label header.\fR The
header for the row\-label column consulted only \f(CW\*(C`row.names\*(C'\fR, so
\&\f(CW\*(C`row_names => \*(Aqid\*(Aq\*(C'\fR displayed \f(CW\*(C`row_name\*(C'\fR instead of \f(CW\*(C`id\*(C'\fR. Both spellings are
now honoured consistently.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBMalformed nested values degrade gracefully.\fR A Hash\-of\-Arrays column or
Hash\-of\-Hashes row whose value is not actually an array/hash reference now
renders as empty cells rather than throwing a dereference error.
.PP
Performance
.IX Subsection "Performance"
.IP \(bu 4
Column gathering no longer sorts once per scanned row. Unique column names
are collected across the scanned rows and sorted a single time (same output
order), and the ellipsis length is computed once rather than per cell.
.PP
Tests
.IX Subsection "Tests"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\f(CW\*(C`t/view.t\*(C'\fR is self\-contained (the \f(CW\*(C`view\*(C'\fR implementation is inlined; it loads
no other files) and covers the new argument handling, the bug fixes above,
and the existing AoH / HoA / HoH behaviour, alignment, truncation, and
output\-path handling.
.PP
\fR\f(CI\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR\fI\fR
.IX Subsection "wilcox_test"
.PP
Corrected four bugs in the \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test\*(C'\fR XSUB plus a portability fix in its exact signed\-rank helper. Behaviour on valid input is unchanged: the R\-agreement cases (unpaired \f(CW\*(C`W = 58\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p = 0.13292\*(C'\fR; paired one\-sided \f(CW\*(C`V = 40\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p = 0.019531\*(C'\fR; separated exact \f(CW\*(C`W = 0\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`p = 0.028571\*(C'\fR) all still match R\*(Aqs \f(CW\*(C`wilcox.test\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Bug fixes
.IX Subsection "Bug fixes"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBInvalid \fR\f(CB\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fR\fB is now rejected.\fR Any value other than \f(CW\*(C`less\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`greater\*(C'\fR previously fell through to the two\-sided branch and returned a two\-sided result mislabelled with the bad string, so a typo like \f(CW\*(C`alternative => "twosided"\*(C'\fR silently "worked". It now croaks unless \f(CW\*(C`alternative\*(C'\fR is one of \f(CW\*(C`two.sided\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`less\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`greater\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBZero/negative variance is guarded.\fR When every observation is tied the approximation\*(Aqs variance collapses to 0 and the old code divided by \f(CWsqrt(0)\fR: \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test([5,5,5], [5,5,5])\*(C'\fR returned \f(CW\*(C`p = 0\*(C'\fR (a "significant" difference between identical samples). It now warns and returns \f(CW\*(C`p = 1\*(C'\fR.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBTwo\-sided continuity correction at \fR\f(CB\*(C`z = 0\*(C'\fR\fB.\fR R uses \f(CW\*(C`sign(z) * 0.5\*(C'\fR, so the correction is \f(CW0\fR when the statistic sits exactly on its mean; the old code used \f(CW\-0.5\fR. Example: \f(CW\*(C`wilcox_test([1,4], [2,3], exact => 0)\*(C'\fR changed from \f(CW\*(C`p = 0.698535\*(C'\fR to \f(CW\*(C`p = 1\*(C'\fR (matches R).
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`exp\*(C'\fR\fB no longer shadows libm.\fR The local \f(CW\*(C`exp\*(C'\fR accumulator (mean of the statistic) shadowed the C library \f(CWexp()\fR; renamed to \f(CW\*(C`mean_w\*(C'\fR (two\-sample) and \f(CW\*(C`mean_v\*(C'\fR (signed\-rank). No active miscompute, removed as a latent hazard.
.PP
Cosmetic
.IX Subsection "Cosmetic"
.IP \(bu 4
Collapsed a no\-op ternary that assigned the same signed\-rank exact method string on both branches; the \f(CW\*(C`method\*(C'\fR field is now simply \f(CW\*(C`Wilcoxon signed rank exact test\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Portability (exact signed\-rank helper)
.IX Subsection "Portability (exact signed-rank helper)"
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`exact_psignrank\*(C'\fR\fB no longer calls \fR\f(CBpowl()\fR\fB.\fR The \f(CW\*(C`2^n\*(C'\fR normaliser is now built by exact repeated doubling, which has no long\-double libm dependency. This fixes an \f(CW\*(C`Undefined symbol "powl"\*(C'\fR load failure reported by a CPAN smoker (FreeBSD, perl 5.20, \f(CW\*(C`nvtype=double\*(C'\fR) whose libm lacks the long\-double math functions; the symbol resolved on glibc, which is why local builds passed. \f(CW\*(C`long double\*(C'\fR accumulation in the DP is retained — only the \f(CW\*(C`powl\*(C'\fR call was at fault.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fR\f(CB\*(C`int\*(C'\fR\fB → \fR\f(CB\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR\fB\fR for \f(CW\*(C`n\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`max_v\*(C'\fR, and the DP loop counters, which also removes a \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR\-to\-\f(CW\*(C`int\*(C'\fR narrowing at the call site. The \f(CWfloor()\fR result (\f(CW\*(C`k\*(C'\fR) stays signed so its negative\-\f(CW\*(C`q\*(C'\fR sentinel still fires, and is cast to \f(CW\*(C`size_t\*(C'\fR only after the \f(CW\*(C`k < 0\*(C'\fR check.
.PP
